@meta
id: 13-17
title: The same thing again
chapter: 13
type: lesson
skills: regex-groups, regex-verymagic
par: 28
minutes: 6
vscode: read it aloud and hope you notice
open: RUNBOOK.md

@brief
This runbook has been edited by four people and proofread by none. Three
times, a word is written twice in a row — "the the", that kind of thing.
Delete the second copy in each case. Everything else stays, including
"the theory", which is not a doubled word, and the code samples.

You cannot search for the doubled word because you do not know which
word it is. What you know is the *relationship*: some word, a space,
and then that same word again. A group captures what it matched, and a
pattern can refer back to its own capture.

@keys
| `\1` | whatever the first group matched, again |
| `\v<(\w+) \1>` | a word, a space, the same word — a doubled word |
| `\2` … `\9` | the second group onward |
| `dw` | delete a word and the space after it |

@hints
Describe it as "a word, then that word". The second half of the
pattern has to be the first half's *result*, not its shape.
--
Round brackets capture. `\1` inside the pattern means "the text the
first group captured". Word anchors on both ends stop `the theory`
from qualifying.
--
`/\v<(\w+) \1>` `Enter` lands on the first copy. `dw` removes it and
the space. `n.` twice for the other two.

@solution
/\v<(\w+) \1><Enter>  dw  n.  n.  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-four keystrokes for a proofreading pass that would take a person
two full readings to do reliably.

Groups capture as well as fence. Every `(...)` in a pattern remembers
what it matched, numbered from the left, and `\1` to `\9` inside the
same pattern mean "that text, again, exactly". This is a
*backreference*, and it is the one thing a pattern can do that a
description of shape cannot: relate two parts of the match to each
other. A doubled word. A quote that has to close with the kind of quote
it opened with — `\v(['"]).{-}\1`. A closing tag that has to match its
opening tag.

Read the anchors carefully because the pattern is nothing without them.
`<(\w+) \1>` requires a word boundary before the first copy and after
the second. Without them, `the theory` matches — `the` then space then
`the` — and so does `in inside`. With them, the second copy has to be a
complete word.

Chapter 14 uses the same captures on the other side of a substitute,
where `\1` in the replacement means "put the captured text here". It is
the same numbering and the same brackets, so the idea will already be
in your hands.

@start RUNBOOK.md
# Payments runbook

## When a payout batch fails

Check the the SFTP host first. Nine times out of ten it is unreachable
because the certificate on the finance side has expired, and the theory
that our exporter is at fault has never once been right.

    ./scripts/payout-status --batch pyt_402

If the batch is stuck in `EXPORTING`, requeue it. Do not delete it: the
ledger has already recorded the debit and and the reconciliation job
will flag the gap.

## When refunds are declined

A decline from the gateway is final. Retry only on `GATEWAY_TIMEOUT`,
never on `PAYMENT_DECLINED` — a retried decline is is a chargeback
waiting to happen.

    ./scripts/refund-retry --order ord_9125 --reason GATEWAY_TIMEOUT

## Escalation

Infra own the SFTP host and the queue broker. Everything else is ours.

